        {
          "id": "the_output_row_carries_its_keys_and_leaves_the_absent_ones_absent",
          "description": "PC-6's key columns on the same rows. The day type, fuel type and model year come from joins to the day and cohort relations on their ordinals; the SCC is COMPUTED as 22e8 + fuel x 1e6 + sourceType x 1e4 + roadType x 1e2 + process, giving 2201210411 for gasoline and 2205210411 for E85 -- the same two SCCs as the leaks slice with a different process suffix; and `regClassID` is absent, which the indicator reads as 1 while the same template on the present `emissionQuant` reads 0. What breaks if this is wrong: the comparator treats SCC as an identity column, so a hard-coded value would be right for the 82 gasoline rows and come back as 46 missing plus 46 extra keys for the E85 ones rather than as a wrong number. The absence cannot be pinned directly because `Assertion.expected` is a JSON number and a NaN actual fails every tolerance form (docs/esm-conventions.md 18.2).",
